# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own
# Subprocess_Driver_Agent_Main (process_agents/process/subprocess_driver_agent.py),
# a standalone action -- expand every top-level process step into its own
# detailed level-2/3 subprocess -- from whatever process JSON is ALREADY
# saved on disk. NO re-analysis, NO process regeneration, and the saved
# process_data.json itself is never modified (each step's own expanded
# subprocess flow is written to its own file under output/subprocesses/
# instead). This is deliberately NOT process.hocon's Process_Pipeline run
# with its early steps skipped -- that network has no entry point that
# starts midway through, and this exact gap was self-documented in
# process_architect.hocon's own "KNOWN GAPS" section before this file
# closed it.
#
# Subprocess_Driver_Agent_Main/Subprocess_Generator_Agent_Main below are
# functionally identical to process.hocon's own Subprocess_Driver_Agent/
# Subprocess_Generator_Agent (same instructions, same load_process_steps/
# save_subprocess_flow tools -- genuinely shared CodedTool classes, not
# re-implemented) -- neuro-san has no mechanism to reuse one network's own
# internal sub-agent from a DIFFERENT network (only a whole network's own
# front-man is reachable externally, via "/network_name"), so duplicating
# the agent INSTRUCTIONS here (while reusing the same underlying tool
# classes) is the only way to expose this standalone. The "_Main" suffix
# mirrors the ADK port's own naming (which distinguishes its root-level
# standalone instance from the one embedded inside its CREATE pipeline)
# and avoids a name collision in this project's own /agents/<name>/
# instructions index (cli.py), which assumes a unique agent name
# project-wide.
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Expands every top-level step of the current business process into its own detailed level-2/3 subprocess, from whatever process JSON is already saved on disk -- no re-analysis, no process regeneration, just subprocess expansion.",
        "tags": ["process", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Please regenerate the subprocesses.",
            "Regenerate the subprocesses for the vendor onboarding process."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Subprocess_Driver_Agent_Main",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I expand every top-level step of the current business process into its own detailed level-2/3
subprocess, from whatever process JSON is already saved on disk. I never re-run analysis, process
design, compliance, or simulation, and I never modify the saved process_data.json itself -- each
step's own expanded subprocess flow is written to its own file instead. Tell me to regenerate the
subprocesses.
"""
            },

            "command": "Drive subprocess expansion now.",
            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You drive subprocess expansion for every top-level step in the CURRENT process design, already
saved on disk. You NEVER re-run analysis, process design, compliance review, or simulation, and you
NEVER modify the saved process_data.json itself -- that is every other network's job, on a separate
request.

# SEQUENCE
1. Call load_process_steps to get the current "process_steps" list.
2. If the list is empty (or no process has been saved yet), report in plain language that there are
   no process steps to expand, and that the user should create a process first (e.g. "create a
   process for ..."), then stop.
3. Otherwise, for EACH step in that list, IN ORDER: call Subprocess_Generator_Agent_Main, passing
   that one step's full object as the "process_step" argument. Subprocess_Generator_Agent_Main
   persists its own result itself -- you do not need to do anything with its reply except move on
   to the next step. Call it once per step, never skipping or batching multiple steps into one call.
4. After every step has been processed, report in plain language that subprocess generation is
   complete.

Do not output JSON or step data as chat text -- it belongs only inside tool calls.
""",
            "tools": ["load_process_steps", "Subprocess_Generator_Agent_Main"]
        },

        {
            "name": "Subprocess_Generator_Agent_Main",
            "function": {
                "description": "Expands a single top-level process step into a detailed level-2/3 subprocess of 3-10 ordered sub-steps.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "process_step": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "The single top-level process step to expand (step_name, description, responsible_party, inputs, outputs, success_criteria, etc.)."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["process_step"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You are an expert process engineer. Given a single top-level process step (the "process_step"
argument -- step_name, description, responsible_party, outputs, inputs, success_criteria, etc.),
expand it into a detailed level-2/3 subprocess.

# RULES
Break the step into 3-10 ordered sub-steps a delivery team could actually implement. For EACH
sub-step, provide (infer a reasonable value if the input doesn't state one -- never leave a field
out):
- substep_name: concise but descriptive (avoid vague names like "Execution" -- use "Validate Code
  Quality" instead). Keep tight if the parent step_name is already long.
- description: a professional, multi-sentence paragraph explaining the action.
- responsible_party: a role aligned with stakeholders identified in the parent process.
- inputs / outputs: lists of artifacts or information. Every output must be either an input to a
  later sub-step or match a top-level output from the parent step.
- estimated_duration: a human-friendly string (e.g. "2-4 hours", "1 day").
- dependencies: names of prior sub-steps that must complete first.
- success_criteria: list of conditions for completion.
- purpose: why this sub-step exists.
- scope: boundaries/limits of this sub-step.
- process_owner: accountable role.
- triggers: events/conditions that initiate it.
- end_conditions: conditions defining completion.
- step_risks_and_controls: list of {"risk", "control"} objects.
- governance_requirements: list of oversight/audit/compliance requirements.
- change_management: list of {"change_request_process", "versioning_rules"} objects.
- continuous_improvement: list of {"review_frequency", "improvement_inputs": [...]} objects.

# COMPLETION
Call save_subprocess_flow with step_name=<the parent step's step_name> and subprocess_flow=
{"step_name": <the parent step's step_name>, "subprocess_flow": [<your list of sub-step objects
above>]}. If it returns "status": "ERROR" with an "issues" list, every named sub-step is missing a
required field (substep_name, description, purpose, scope, process_owner, or responsible_party --
a missing substep_name is what makes the final document render a generic "Sub-step N" heading
instead of a real one; a too-short description reads as a one-line summary rather than the
multi-sentence explanation the document needs) -- fix exactly those fields and call
save_subprocess_flow again; repeat until it returns "status": "OK".

Do not output the subprocess JSON as chat text -- it belongs only inside the tool call.
""",
            "tools": ["save_subprocess_flow"]
        },

        {
            "name": "load_process_steps",
            "function": {"description": "Loads the current process design's top-level process_steps list. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "subprocess_tool.LoadProcessStepsCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "save_subprocess_flow",
            "function": {
                "description": "Persists a generated subprocess flow for one top-level process step.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "step_name": {"type": "string", "description": "The parent process step's step_name."},
                        "subprocess_flow": {"type": "object", "description": "{\"step_name\": ..., \"subprocess_flow\": [<sub-step objects>]}"}
                    },
                    "required": ["step_name", "subprocess_flow"]
                }
            },
            "class": "subprocess_tool.SaveSubprocessFlowCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
